oldlibc - the Jan-1983 libc revision embedded in size, strip and ranlib
=======================================================================

readsym.c  _readsym as embedded in ranlib (0x724..0x79b of /usr/bin/ranlib). It reads the
           pieces of a long symbol name over the symbol (no "sym++"), which the shipped libc.a
           readsym does not; ranlib's __.SYMDEF depends on that. Same code as the original,
           but compiled by the shipped cc (other register use, frame 2 bytes larger).

doprnt.c   _doprnt (+ fix, getnum, float stubs) reconstructed from the
           object code of bin/size (0x4ec..0xb54).  UNTESTED, NOT
           BYTE-IDENTICAL (see below); the control flow, local variable
           layout and jump tables follow the original.

Why this directory does not produce byte-identical size/strip:

 * The members were compiled with an OLDER cc revision than the one
   shipped in /bin/cc.  Visible differences: post-increment through a
   pointer in a store (`*p->_ptr++ = c`) is emitted as load/store/inc
   by the old cc and as inc/load/dec by the shipped cc; the varargs
   prologue (registers spilled in reverse order into one contiguous
   argument block, f3..f0 spilled with inline EPU words) is a feature
   of the old cc only; char loads are `ldb`+`subb` vs `ldk`+`ldb`.
 * No copy of the old compiler is available (all archives contain the
   same 1990-03-01 cparse/codgen/gopt/popt).

So printf/fprintf/sprintf (which rely on the old varargs prologue) are
not reconstructed as C; only the code that the shipped cc can express.
